(interesting that the ZJIT team decided to write a paper about it: Support Local Variables) Tj
T*
(\(PDF\). It?s also the first bit of academic literature that documents and explains ZJIT. It?s) Tj
T*
(published at VMIL 2026, where someone on our team will be presenting it. Abstract Ruby is a) Tj
T*
(dynamically typed and object-oriented programming language. Its primary implementation, CRuby,) Tj
T*
(contains a bytecode virtual machine and a mature lazy basic block versioning \(LBBV\)) Tj
T*
(just-in-time \(JIT\) compiler called YJIT. In order to both implement more advanced optimizations) Tj
T*
(than YJIT supports and also encourage more outside contributions, we present a new method-based) Tj
T*
(JIT called ZJIT. Like YJIT, ZJIT compiles from bytecode to machine code. Unlike YJIT, ZJIT has) Tj
T*
(multiple global and local optimization passes. ZJIT?s high-level intermediate representation is) Tj
T*
(in static single assignment \(SSA\) form. In order to optimize Ruby?s local variables, ZJIT lifts) Tj
T*
(local variables into SSA values. This is a departure from how other Ruby compilers handle) Tj
T*
(locals: other JIT compilers either leave local variables as memory loads and stores or d...) Tj
T*
